#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079457} :activity/id #uuid "45c505a4-630e-59ff-9596-9a36627fb33e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050396 :topic/id #uuid "457136ef-67f9-50bb-b078-34de9c6b59a9" :topic/math-academy-id 655} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079621 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial term** is a term where the variable contains a non-negative integer power. (The non-negative integers consist of the numbers $0$, $1$, $2$, $3$, and so on.)\n\nSome examples of polynomial terms include\n\n$$\nx^2, \\qquad \\dfrac 1 3 x^6,\\qquad x, \\qquad 1\n$$\n\nLet's go through each one:\n\n- $x^{\\color{blue}2}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}2}$, a non-negative integer.\n- $\\dfrac{1}{3} x^{\\color{blue}6}$ is a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}6}$, which is also a non-negative integer. The coefficient of $\\dfrac 1 3$ isn't important.\n- $x$ is a polynomial term because it can be thought of as $x^{\\color{blue}1}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}1}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n- Finally, $1$ is a polynomial term because it can be expressed as $1\\cdot x^{\\color{blue}0}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{blue}0}$, which is a non-negative integer.\n\nOn the other hand, the following expressions are *not* polynomial terms because their powers are not non-negative integers:\n\n$$\n4x^{-2}, \\qquad x^{1/3}, \\qquad {\\sqrt{\\color{black}x}}, \\qquad {\\dfrac{\\color{black}1}{x^3}}\n$$\n\nTo see why, let's go through each one:\n\n- $4x^{\\color{red}-2}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-2}$, which is negative.\n- $x^{\\color{red}1/3}$ is not a polynomial term because the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/3}$, which is not an integer.\n- $\\sqrt x$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{\\color{red}1/2}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}1/2}$, which is not an integer.\n- Finally, $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ is not a polynomial term because it can be written as $x^{-\\color{red}3}$. So the power of the variable is ${\\color{red}-3}$, which is negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "a11e9fb3-6fe1-547c-996f-fb83d15a47d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1578 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0500aa37-469a-53bd-a5da-8fbb0fe569cc" :step/math-academy-id 4106 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078529 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078153 :question/id #uuid "0690af9e-ae28-50a0-b2fd-0a9c3baaec3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1280"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c12f648d-f276-5123-b6aa-b222ad108825" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050495}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079149}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polynomial Terms"} :step/id #uuid "ad32752d-72d3-5211-adbf-f50f0a51075b" :step/math-academy-id 4107 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079459}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079622 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial** is a sum of polynomial terms, such as\n$x^2 -5x +3, \\qquad x^3 + x^2 - 9x, \\qquad 4x^4 -2x^2, \\qquad 3x^6 - 2x^3+x^2-1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b356365-33dd-5603-8027-95f09e3184a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1579 :tutorial/title "Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "a43ff34d-c027-5df2-820c-70b022c83dc9" :step/math-academy-id 4109 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078530 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078155 :question/id #uuid "f6c0be28-9441-513e-83c5-866292fd307a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df8ab577-24c5-5ac4-a9de-9ecc7dfb6653" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079069}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "2556eb4e-afab-5ae3-8996-7aa3b8f721fc" :step/math-academy-id 4112 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079623 :tutorial/content "To **evaluate** a polynomial at a specific value of $x$, we substitute the value of $x$ into the polynomial and simplify.\n\nFor example, to evaluate the polynomial $2x^2+3$ at $x=5$, we substitute $x=5$ into the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(5)^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(25) + 3 &=  \\\\\n50 + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 53\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d8de8488-40d3-593a-a8a1-9193eb94f8e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1580 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "64a56fd4-dcbc-5271-8a10-da5817638249" :step/math-academy-id 4114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078531 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078162 :question/id #uuid "5242547e-f74d-541d-8c3b-def606b70cb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1283"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f7a2ec-3d90-5930-bcc0-d51b8111dbe7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480647}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "f4dd171c-5f47-5387-8772-afefbec23a35" :step/math-academy-id 4115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078532 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078163 :question/id #uuid "7dc04a57-3d13-5482-93c2-db2cee4ca41a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1284"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2229bd-c425-5e53-b8cf-56f3f9f3f539" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233572}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Polynomial Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "666b2e7a-bdbf-55bd-a1ea-25b21b4d56f8" :step/math-academy-id 4117}] :activity/title "Introduction to Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078677}]}
